# tcpdrill, RFC 5961, the blind in-window attacks.
#
# RFC 793 lets a segment that lands anywhere in the receive window decide the
# fate of the connection: a RESET tears it down, and so does a SYN.  The window
# is tens of kilobytes wide, so an attacker who knows the four-tuple has to
# guess one number out of a few thousand rather than one out of four billion.
# RFC 5961 narrows that to a single number for the RESET and removes it
# altogether for the SYN.
#
# THE ANSWER IN BOTH CASES IS A CHALLENGE ACKNOWLEDGMENT: an ordinary ACK
# carrying SND.NXT and RCV.NXT, which is byte-identical to the last one this
# side sent.  A peer that really did lose the connection answers it with a
# RESET carrying the sequence number the challenge named, and that one is
# honoured, n06 is that exchange end to end, and is what keeps this from
# being a refusal to close.
#
# NONE OF THESE CASES HAS EVER BEEN RUN.
#
# WHAT IS NOT ASSERTED HERE.  RFC 5961 section 3's motivating case is a RESET
# arriving while RCV.WND is 0, which RFC 793's acceptability test admits at any
# sequence number at all.  Filling this stack's receive window takes between
# 8 KB and 33 KB of unread data (w01 in tcp.drill bounds it) and the exact
# figure is pool-derived, so there is no segment count a script can name.  It
# is the same branch n01 exercises, reached from a different direction.
#
# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT

# RFC 5961 3: a RESET inside the window but not at RCV.NXT is answered with a
# challenge and changes nothing.  50 bytes past the left edge is inside any
# window this stack can advertise.
case n01_off_sequence_rst_is_challenged
socket
connect
tx S seq=0
rx SA seq=0 ack=1 win=8192 mss=1460
tx A seq=1 ack=1
rx R seq=51
tx A seq=1 ack=1 within=400
send 10
tx PA seq=1 ack=1 len=10 within=400

# The control.  A RESET at exactly RCV.NXT is the one RFC 5961 still honours,
# so r03 in tcp.drill keeps its meaning: torn down, and nothing sent back.
case n02_rst_at_rcv_nxt_still_tears_down
socket
connect
tx S seq=0
rx SA seq=0 ack=1 win=8192 mss=1460
tx A seq=1 ack=1
rx R seq=1
notx 200
recv 100 = 0

# RFC 5961 4: a SYN on an established connection is answered with a challenge
# and NOT with a reset, and the connection carries on.
case n03_in_window_syn_is_challenged_not_reset
socket
connect
tx S seq=0
rx SA seq=0 ack=1 win=8192 mss=1460
tx A seq=1 ack=1
rx S seq=51
tx A seq=1 ack=1 within=400
send 10
tx PA seq=1 ack=1 len=10 within=400

# The same at the left edge.  RFC 5961 4 makes no exception for a SYN that
# happens to carry RCV.NXT, which is the sequence number a peer that genuinely
# restarted would not have.
case n04_syn_at_rcv_nxt_is_challenged
socket
connect
tx S seq=0
rx SA seq=0 ack=1 win=8192 mss=1460
tx A seq=1 ack=1
rx S seq=1
tx A seq=1 ack=1 within=400
recv 100 = AGAIN

# A SYN carrying an acknowledgement is the same segment as far as this side is
# concerned, and gets the same answer.
case n05_syn_ack_in_window_is_challenged
socket
connect
tx S seq=0
rx SA seq=0 ack=1 win=8192 mss=1460
tx A seq=1 ack=1
rx SA seq=51 ack=1
tx A seq=1 ack=1 within=400
send 10
tx PA seq=1 ack=1 len=10 within=400

# THE RECOVERY, which is the whole reason a challenge is an adequate answer.
# The peer really did restart: it sends a SYN, is challenged, learns RCV.NXT
# from the challenge, and sends the RESET that the challenge invited.  That one
# is at RCV.NXT, so it is honoured and the connection goes away.
case n06_challenged_peer_closes_with_a_reset
socket
connect
tx S seq=0
rx SA seq=0 ack=1 win=8192 mss=1460
tx A seq=1 ack=1
rx S seq=51
tx A seq=1 ack=1 within=400
rx R seq=1
notx 200
recv 100 = 0

# A RESET the acceptability test rejects outright is still dropped in silence
# the challenge is for segments INSIDE the window, and a segment far past
# the right edge must not draw one.
#
# PAST THE RIGHT EDGE IS A DERIVED PLACE, not a number.  RCV.NXT is 1 here and
# the window is $rwnd, read off the ACK above, so anything beyond $rwnd+1 is
# outside it and $rwnd+10000 is clear of it by any margin.  This line used to
# say 40001, chosen as "beyond the widest window w01 allows" when that was
# 33580: on a build whose window is 50176 the segment is INSIDE the window,
# RFC 5961 4 says to challenge it, the stack correctly sent the challenge ACK,
# and the case failed on the constant rather than on the behaviour.
case n07_out_of_window_rst_draws_nothing
socket
connect
tx S seq=0
rx SA seq=0 ack=1 win=8192 mss=1460
tx A seq=1 ack=1
rx R seq=$rwnd+10000
notx 400
send 10
tx PA seq=1 ack=1 len=10 within=400
